\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Post-rifting displacement profiles across the Krafla rift and the diffusion models published with them, reproduced without refitting. (a) \citet  {foulger1992post}, their Figure~2b: displacements of 20 stations between 1987 and 1990. (b) \citet  {hofton1996post}, their Figure~5: displacements of 17 stations between 1987 and 1992. Error bars are $1\sigma $. Red curves: the best-fit diffusion model of each study, $u(x,t) = (b/2)\,\mathrm  {erfc}[x/(2\sqrt  {\kappa t})]$, written here in terms of the full dike opening $b$ (both studies report the dike half-width $U_0 = b/2$). Following \citet  {foulger1992post}, each profile is modelled as the velocity of this solution at a time $t$ after the intrusions, multiplied by the survey interval, with $t = 11$~yr in (a) and $t = 13$~yr in (b), as each study assumes. Published parameters: (a) $b = 2.0$~m ($U_0 = 1.0 \pm 0.1$~m) and $\kappa = 1.1 \pm 0.3$~m$^2$~s$^{-1}$; (b) $b = 2.54$~m ($U_0 = 1.27 \pm 0.14$~m) and $\kappa = 3.11 \pm 0.68$~m$^2$~s$^{-1}$. The zero reference of profile (b) is arbitrary \citep  {hofton1996post}, so its curve is shifted by the constant 6.7~cm read from their Figure~16, not fitted. Both curves return to a single constant level far from the rift, whereas the far-field stations of (b) settle on distinct levels on the two flanks, below the curve to the west and above it to the east.}}{3}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:hofton_diffusion}{{1}{3}{Post-rifting displacement profiles across the Krafla rift and the diffusion models published with them, reproduced without refitting. (a) \citet {foulger1992post}, their Figure~2b: displacements of 20 stations between 1987 and 1990. (b) \citet {hofton1996post}, their Figure~5: displacements of 17 stations between 1987 and 1992. Error bars are $1\sigma $. Red curves: the best-fit diffusion model of each study, $u(x,t) = (b/2)\,\mathrm {erfc}[x/(2\sqrt {\kappa t})]$, written here in terms of the full dike opening $b$ (both studies report the dike half-width $U_0 = b/2$). Following \citet {foulger1992post}, each profile is modelled as the velocity of this solution at a time $t$ after the intrusions, multiplied by the survey interval, with $t = 11$~yr in (a) and $t = 13$~yr in (b), as each study assumes. Published parameters: (a) $b = 2.0$~m ($U_0 = 1.0 \pm 0.1$~m) and $\kappa = 1.1 \pm 0.3$~m$^2$~s$^{-1}$; (b) $b = 2.54$~m ($U_0 = 1.27 \pm 0.14$~m) and $\kappa = 3.11 \pm 0.68$~m$^2$~s$^{-1}$. The zero reference of profile (b) is arbitrary \citep {hofton1996post}, so its curve is shifted by the constant 6.7~cm read from their Figure~16, not fitted. Both curves return to a single constant level far from the rift, whereas the far-field stations of (b) settle on distinct levels on the two flanks, below the curve to the west and above it to the east}{figure.1}{}}
